            <ArticleTitle>Setting up a clinic to assess children and young people for female genital mutilation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>14-18</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/archdischild-2016-311296</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>It is now mandatory for health, social care professionals and teachers to report to the police all under-18s where female genital mutilation (FGM) has been disclosed by the child or where physical signs of FGM are seen. Such referrals are likely to result in a request for medical examination. New multiagency statutory guidance sets out instructions for physical examination but provides no details how services should be set-up. This review gives practical guidance learnt from the first year of the UK's only dedicated children's FGM service.</AbstractText>
